     6	- Subscription entitlement resolution is a store-level deterministic layering rule: current subscriptions are `trialing|active|past_due` and inside `current_period_start <= at < current_period_end`; canceled/out-of-period subscriptions contribute no runtime entitlements; candidate precedence is plan defaults < `subscription_override` rows for that subscription < organization-level `emergency_admin` rows, with newest active `effective_from`, then `created_at`, then `id` as tie-breakers. Override reasons are redacted before persistence.
     7	- Pricing plan catalog rows are versioned, global configuration rather than tenant data: `plans` uses immutable accepted versions keyed by `(slug, billing_period, version)` plus one active version per `(slug, billing_period)`, and `PricingPlanRepository.Update` archives the addressed row before inserting the next version and cloning entitlements. Do not update an accepted plan row in place.
     8	- Database engine drift is a service subtype mismatch in reconciliation: when both desired and actual are `dokploy.ServiceDatabase` but `Engine` differs after trim/case normalization, emit one dangerous `ActionReviewServiceTypeChange` / `ReasonServiceTypeChanged` action and return before env/domain safe repairs. This prevents auto-mutating a database resource whose storage engine no longer matches Yalla source of truth.
     9	- Application/compose runtime role drift is a service subtype mismatch in reconciliation: when desired projects a non-empty role and actual reports a different role, emit one dangerous `ActionReviewServiceRoleChange` / `ReasonServiceRoleChanged` action and stop before build/env/domain safe repairs. For non-web roles such as worker and cron, desired domains are ignored by the planner so a bad adapter cannot auto-bind inbound HTTP to a background process.
